# Local Development and Secrets Rule

All applications deployed via the platform must work locally without any Azure resources. See the `platform-resource-config` rule for which infrastructure resources are available and their specific setup instructions.

## ⚠️ If this project has 3rd-party connections — MCP is STEP 0

If the project includes any connection-specific rules (in `.cursor/rules/` for Cursor, or merged into `AGENTS.md` for Claude Code) or skill folders for a connection slug, it uses the VibeOps MCP server for local credentials. **Before** you run `npm run dev`, `pnpm dev`, `python manage.py runserver`, or any other dev command, you MUST follow the connection-specific rule for that slug.

For BackOffice connections specifically, see `backoffice-api-rules.mdc` STEP 0 — credentials are loaded by `node scripts/vibeops-dev.mjs <slug>`, which itself spawns `npm run dev`. **Do NOT** call `mcp_vibeops_get_credentials` from the agent surface; **do NOT** shell-export `BACKOFFICE_*=...`; **do NOT** check whether `.cursor/mcp.json` exists in the project root as a precondition (the install script writes the user-global `~/.cursor/mcp.json`, not a project-local file — the project-local file is supposed to be absent).

For Databricks (delegated) connections specifically, see `azure-databricks-delegated-connection.mdc` and the `databricks` skill — credentials are loaded the same way, by `node scripts/vibeops-dev.mjs <slug>` (which sets `DATABRICKS_*` in the dev-server child and spawns `npm run dev`). **Do NOT** call `mcp_vibeops_get_credentials` from the agent surface; **do NOT** shell-export `DATABRICKS_*=...`; **do NOT** use a Databricks PAT — the user's AAD token is the credential.

For other connection types without a dedicated rule/script:

1. Read the connection slugs from the project (check the connection-specific rules like `*-connection.mdc`, or call `mcp_vibeops_list_connections`).
2. For each connection, call `mcp_vibeops_get_credentials` with that slug.
3. Export the returned `client_id`, `client_secret`, and (when present) `tenant_id` as environment variables into the same shell you will run the dev server in.
4. Only **after** credentials are exported, start the dev server.

If env vars are missing and the dev server errors out, the fix is **always** to re-call MCP — never to add error handling, placeholder values, `.env` entries, or a "paste credentials" UI. See the connection-specific `*-connection.mdc` rule for the exact variable names to export.

### If the MCP tools are not available in this session

If you cannot see `mcp_vibeops_get_credentials` in your available tools, the VibeOps MCP server is not enabled in your IDE yet (Cursor requires a manual toggle the first time; Claude Code registers it via the installer). **Stop immediately** and show the user this exact message, then wait:

> The VibeOps MCP server is not enabled. To enable it:
>
> **Cursor:**
> 1. Open **Cursor Settings** (`Cmd+Shift+J` on Mac, `Ctrl+Shift+J` on Windows).
> 2. Go to **Tools & MCPs** (or **Features → MCP Servers**).
> 3. Find `vibeops` and toggle it **ON**.
> 4. Wait for the green dot / "Connected" status.
>
> **Claude Code:**
